## Topic Understanding

Starting therapy is an important step toward taking care of your mental health. However, before scheduling your first appointment, you may have questions about insurance coverage, session fees, copayments, and out-of-pocket expenses.

Understanding your mental health benefits can make the process less stressful and help you plan for the cost of counseling. Even when an insurance company offers mental health coverage, the amount you pay may depend on your specific plan, the therapist you choose, the type of service, and whether the provider participates in your insurance network.

If you are considering therapy at Riaz Counseling, knowing what to ask about insurance coverage can help you make informed decisions before beginning treatment.

This guide explains how to check your mental health insurance benefits, which questions to ask the practice and your insurance company, what to expect during benefit verification, and what options may be available if your insurance does not cover your preferred therapist.

## Key Highlights

- Insurance coverage for therapy depends on your specific plan, provider network, covered services, and benefit requirements.
- Riaz Counseling publishes a list of accepted insurance networks, but individual coverage should be verified before scheduling.
- Ask about deductibles, copayments, coinsurance, session limits, and authorization requirements.
- If you do not plan to use insurance, ask about self-pay rates, discounted services, and good faith estimates.
- Confirming your benefits before your first appointment can help reduce unexpected financial surprises.

## 1. Understand How Insurance Coverage for Therapy Works

Health insurance can help reduce the cost of mental health counseling, but understanding your benefits requires looking beyond the name of your insurance company.

Two people with the same insurance provider may have different coverage because they have different plans, networks, deductibles, and benefit arrangements.

For example, one person may have an insurance plan that covers outpatient therapy with a fixed copayment, while another may need to meet an annual deductible before insurance begins paying its share of eligible services.

### What Does Mental Health Insurance Coverage Include?

Depending on your plan, mental health benefits may include services such as:

- Individual psychotherapy.
- Couples or family counseling when covered under the plan.
- Mental health assessments and evaluations.
- Outpatient treatment for eligible mental health conditions.
- Certain telehealth mental health services.
- Other covered behavioral health services.

The exact services and providers covered depend on your policy and applicable coverage requirements.

Under the Affordable Care Act, Marketplace health plans include mental health and substance use disorder services as essential health benefits. Federal mental health parity protections also apply to many employer-sponsored and individual health plans, requiring covered mental health benefits to be treated comparably to medical and surgical benefits under applicable rules.

However, these protections do not mean every counseling service or every therapist is automatically covered.

You can learn more through the U.S. Department of Labor's mental health parity resources and HealthCare.gov.

### In-Network vs. Out-of-Network Therapists

One of the first things to understand is whether your therapist is in-network or out-of-network with your insurance plan.

**In-network therapists:** These professionals participate in an insurance company's network and have an agreement regarding covered services and reimbursement.

**Out-of-network therapists:** These professionals do not participate in your specific insurance network. Depending on your plan, you may have partial reimbursement available, or you may need to pay the full fee yourself.

Some insurance plans provide out-of-network benefits, while others offer limited or no coverage for out-of-network outpatient counseling.

When contacting a counseling practice, ask whether the specific therapist you want to see participates in your exact plan.

